<!--
  Dutch labels, in BELGIAN usage.

  Belgium's B2B mandate has been in force since January 2026 and the invoice
  language is set by the seller's region: Dutch in Flanders (Taaldecreet, 19 July
  1973), French in Wallonia, German in Ostbelgien, either in Brussels. An invoice
  in the wrong language can be held void, so this is not a convenience.

  WHY BELGIAN AND NOT NETHERLANDS DUTCH. The two differ on exactly the words an
  invoice uses. Belgian practice says "Verkoper" where the Netherlands says
  "Leverancier", and writes the totals as "Totaal (excl. BTW)". Flanders is the
  mandate market, so Belgian usage wins wherever the two disagree. The file still
  renders correctly for a Netherlands reader — the terms are understood there,
  they are simply not the first choice.

  SOURCING. Grounded in (a) the Belgian statutory invoice requirements
  ("verplichte vermeldingen"), which fix the btw vocabulary and the
  excl./incl. btw totals; and (b) Dolibarr's nl_BE translation layered over
  nl_NL, which is how Dolibarr itself resolves Belgian Dutch — nl_BE is a thin
  override file, not a full translation. Terms taken from the nl_BE override
  specifically: Verkoper, Totaal (excl. BTW), E.P., Verminderingen.

  CASING OF "btw". Lowercase inside compounds (btw-nummer, btw-overzicht),
  uppercase standing alone in the totals block (Totaal BTW) — which is what both
  the statutory guidance and Belgian invoices in the wild do.

  STILL WANTS A NATIVE REVIEW (lowest confidence first): lines.broughtfwd,
  totals.rounding, totals.charges, meta.credits. "Overdracht" for a carried
  subtotal is defensible and "Transport" is the older bookkeeping term; a Flemish
  accountant should pick. The PINT A-NZ overrides are a fallback for an unlikely
  combination and are the least attested lines here.
-->
